  "content": {
    "id": "p45-q10-node",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "10",
    "prompt": "A factory produces items whose costs are $\\$1000$ plus $\\$10$ for every item.\nThe factory receives $\\$60$ for every item sold.\nWrite two equations, one for costs and one for revenue.\nFind how many items must be sold for the factory to break-even (cost = revenue).",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$C = 1000 + 10n$, $R = 60n$. Break-even at 20 items.",
      "worked": "Let $n$ be the number of items produced and sold, and let $C$ and $R$ be the cost and revenue in dollars.\n$$\\begin{align*}\nC &= 1000 + 10n \\\\\nR &= 60n\n\\end{align*}$$\nAt break-even, $C = R$.\n$$\\begin{align*}\n1000 + 10n &= 60n \\\\\n50n &= 1000 \\\\\nn &= 20\n\\end{align*}$$\nThe factory must sell 20 items to break even.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 52
  },
